Content:
Suppose we want to plot the graph of the function [MATH: y= \sinh x.] To do this, we start with the definition:

Content:
[MATH: \sinh x = \dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}]

Content:
We create a table of values by substituting some [MATH: x]-values into the definition of [MATH: y = \sinh x] and finding the corresponding (approximate) [MATH: y]-values:

Content:
| [MATH: x] | [MATH: -2] | [MATH: -1.5] | [MATH: -1] | [MATH: -0.5] | [MATH: 0] | [MATH: 0.5] | [MATH: 1] | [MATH: 1.5] | [MATH: 2] |
| [MATH: \sinh x] | [MATH: -3.63] | [MATH: -2.13] | [MATH: -1.18] | [MATH: -0.52] | [MATH: 0] | [MATH: 0.52] | [MATH: 1.18] | [MATH: 2.13] | [MATH: 3.63] |

Content:
Plotting these points, we get the following curve:

Graphic:
[IMG: /graphics/cef48e135449bc8038ee97bd41f1e178]

Content:
The key features of the graph are as follows:

Content:
- The domain and range are [MATH: (-\infty,\infty).]
- It is an odd function.
- It has a zero at [MATH: x=0.]
- Its graph is increasing over the entire real line. Moreover,

[MATH: \qquad] [MATH: \sinh{x} \to -\infty] as [MATH: x \to -\infty,]

[MATH: \qquad] [MATH: \sinh{x} \to \infty] as [MATH: x \to \infty.]
- It is the mean (average) of the functions [MATH: y=e^x] and [MATH: y=-e^{-x}.]

